    <string name="fact_gray_mouse">A mouse can squeeze through a gap as narrow as a pencil.</string>
    <string name="fact_mouselet">Baby mice open their eyes about two weeks after birth.</string>
    <string name="fact_rat">Rats laugh when tickled — in ultrasound humans can\'t hear.</string>
    <string name="fact_white_mouse">White mice are albinos: their eyes are pink because blood vessels show through.</string>
    <string name="fact_sparrow">Sparrows take dust baths to keep their feathers clean.</string>
    <string name="fact_tit">A great tit knows more than 40 different calls.</string>
    <string name="fact_bullfinch">Bullfinches come to towns in winter, which is why they are called “snow birds” in some languages.</string>
    <string name="fact_butterfly">The eyespots on a peacock butterfly\'s wings scare off birds.</string>
    <string name="fact_swallowtail">The swallowtail is one of the largest butterflies of Europe.</string>
    <string name="fact_fly">A fly tastes food with its feet.</string>
    <string name="fact_ladybug">A ladybird can eat up to 5000 aphids in its life.</string>
    <string name="fact_beetle">Ground beetles hunt at night and run surprisingly fast.</string>
    <string name="fact_cricket">Crickets chirp by rubbing their wings, and their ears are on their legs.</string>
    <string name="fact_crucian">Crucian carp can survive winter in ponds with very little oxygen.</string>
    <string name="fact_goldfish">Goldfish were bred in China from a wild carp more than a thousand years ago.</string>
    <string name="fact_tadpole">A tadpole breathes with gills and grows legs before it becomes a frog.</string>
    <string name="fact_water_strider">Water striders walk on water thanks to tiny water-repellent hairs on their legs.</string>
    <string name="fact_firefly">Fireflies glow almost without heat — it is cold light.</string>
    <string name="fact_giant_firefly">Some fireflies flash in sync, lighting up whole trees.</string>

